AIM :

To create a simple Employee Management module using Spring Core, demonstrating Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI) using annotations like @Component and @Autowired, and managing beans using BeanFactory to store employee data in memory.

PROCEDURE :

Create a Spring Core project in Eclipse
Add required dependencies (spring-context)
Create package structure:
model
service
main
Create an Employee class to store employee details
Create a Service class annotated with @Component
Use @Autowired to inject dependencies
Configure applicationContext.xml for component scanning
Use BeanFactory to load beans
Run the main class and display employee data 

Code:

package demo;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.BeanFactory;
import org.springframework.context.support.ClassPathXmlApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;

// -------------------- MODEL --------------------
class Employee {
    private int id;
    private String name;

    public Employee() {}

    public Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("ID: " + id + ", Name: " + name);
    }
}

// -------------------- REPOSITORY --------------------
@Component
class EmployeeRepository {

    public Employee getEmployee() {
        return new Employee(101, "Ganesh");
    }
}

// -------------------- SERVICE --------------------
@Component
class EmployeeService {

    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repo;

    public Employee fetchEmployee() {
        return repo.getEmployee();
    }
}

// -------------------- MAIN --------------------
public class MainApp {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BeanFactory factory =
                new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("AppConfig.xml");

        EmployeeService service =
                factory.getBean(EmployeeService.class);

        Employee emp = service.fetchEmployee();
        emp.display();
    }
}

RESULT

Thus, a simple Employee Management module was successfully created using Spring Core.
Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI) were demonstrated using @Component, and beans were managed using BeanFactory. Employee data was stored and retrieved successfully in memory.

